    {
      "name": "vaulted_hall",
      "weight": 12,
      // OFF THE MUD FLOOR (Gottsch, 2026-08-29). The stone halls are gated to floor 1 and below so
      // floor 0 -- the mud stratum -- rolls only `plain` and the mud band's own `mud_pit_trap`,
      // which is what makes that depth designable and testable without stone rooms leaking into
      // every sample.
      //
      // A PER-SCHEME GATE, NOT A MOVE INTO A BAND. A band's `schemes` MERGES onto the motif's and
      // sections do not inherit from the band above, so schemes moved onto the floor-1 band would
      // leave floors 2+ with nothing but `plain` -- covering both means writing them twice. The
      // gate says the same thing once, and RoomSchemeSelector already reads it (`fitsFloor`).
      //
      // IT KEYS ON FLOOR INDEX, NOT ON THE STRATUM. The two coincide only because mud is floor 0
      // today; move that band and these gates do not follow it. Delete the `minFloorIndex` lines
      // to put the halls back on every floor.
      "min_floor_index": 1,
      "min_size": 9,
      "min_height": 7,

      // A SUNKEN FLOOR (backlog #3). PROBE CONTENT, retuned 2026-08-27 after walking the first
      // version: it was 3 deep and read as a stepped hole rather than a sunken floor. Mark:
      // "it probably should only sink down 1 and ringed with stair blocks".
      //
      // ONE BLOCK DOWN, and the stairs are what make that a step you can see rather than a kerb
      // you trip off. The ring is drawn on the floor cells just OUTSIDE the court, at the room's
      // own walking plane, so a player descends floor -> the stair's low half -> the sunken floor.
      // Those cells stay walkable and are not claimed.
      //
      // COBBLESTONE STAIRS because the band's floor is cobblestone: this is the same paving
      // stepping down, not a different structure set into it. The mud band's own floor is
      // cobblestone too, so it reads the same at both depths.
      //
      // `rimOrient` names where a VANILLA stair's solid half ends up. `outward` puts it away from
      // the court, so the low edge meets the court. dungeonblocks' trim is modelled
      // facing-inverted, so a dungeonblocks stair here would want `inward` for the same look.
      //
      // ON THIS SCHEME because it is the only shipped one with NEITHER `pillars` NOR `platforms`
      // -- a column standing in an excavated cell would hang over the hole (backlog #58). Props
      // are safe: the excavated cells are claimed, so chests, spawners and pots keep off.
      "pit": {
        "type": "dungeons2:centre",
        "config": {
          "size": 5,
          "depth": 1,
          "rim_block": "minecraft:cobblestone_stairs",
          "rim_orient": "outward"
        }
      },
      "ceiling": {
        "patterns": [
          {
            "type": "dungeons2:border",
            "projection": 2,
            "config": {
              "block": "minecraft:stone_brick_stairs",
              "inset": 0,
              "orient": "outward",
              "properties": {
                "half": "top"
              }
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "dungeons2:border",
            "projection": 1,
            "config": {
              "block": "minecraft:stone_bricks",
              "inset": 0
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "dungeons2:border",
            "projection": 1,
            "config": {
              "block": "minecraft:stone_brick_stairs",
              "inset": 1,
              "orient": "outward",
              "properties": {
                "half": "top"
              }
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      "wall": {
        "patterns": [
          {
            "type": "dungeons2:courses",
            "config": {
              "courses": [
                {
                  "block": "minecraft:polished_andesite",
                  "corner_block": "minecraft:andesite",
                  "anchor": "top"
                }
              ]
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      "spawners": {
        "min_count": 0,
        "max_count": 1
      },
      "chests": {
        "min_count": 0,
        "max_count": 1,
        "variants": [
          {
            "block": "minecraft:chest",
            "weight": 1
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      // DEEP VAULT -- three corbelled steps rather than vaulted_hall's two. Backlog #28b, which
      // raised the ceiling's projection cap from 2 to 4 by giving it its own constant instead of
      // borrowing the wall's. This is the first scheme that spends the new depth.
      //
      // WHY minSize 11 AND minHeight 8, AND WHY BOTH ARE LOAD-BEARING.
      // Two constraints pull in opposite directions and this gate is where they meet.
      //
      //   FOOTPRINT. Each ring steps one cell inward, so N rings eat 2N cells of interior. A vault
      //   has to FRAME a field, not consume it -- vaulted_hall's own note records that at minSize 7
      //   its two rings left a 1x1 apex and read as a stepped pyramid rather than as a vault. Three
      //   rings eat 6, so the interior must be at least 9 to leave a 3x3 field, and interior 9 means
      //   a room of 11. That is the floor on size, and it is why this cannot simply be vaulted_hall
      //   with one more ring.
      //
      //   HEIGHT, AND THE TAPER RUNS THE WRONG WAY. The #51 taper makes the ceiling FALL as the
      //   footprint grows -- long side <=7 gives maxHeight 10, <=11 gives 9, <=15 gives 8, larger
      //   gives 7. So the big rooms a dome wants are exactly the short ones, and a bigger footprint
      //   buys field at the cost of the headroom the extra step needs.
      //
      // minHeight 8 is what resolves it, and it does more work than it looks. SizeGate matches on
      // the SHORT side (Math.min) while the height taper matches on the LONG side (Math.max), so a
      // size gate cannot name a taper band directly. It does not have to: any room whose long side
      // is 17+ sits in the open-ended band capped at maxHeight 7 and therefore CANNOT roll 8, so
      // minHeight 8 excludes every room too flat to carry three steps without naming a single
      // dimension. What survives is 11x11 at height 8-9 and 11x13 / 11x15 at height 8.
      //
      // CLEARANCE, CHECKED RATHER THAN ASSUMED. Interior rows are height - 2, and the vault eats
      // three of them at the perimeter: at height 8 that leaves 3 rows of clear wall, which is
      // exactly what vaulted_hall's two-step leaves at its own minHeight 7, and at height 9 it
      // leaves 4. So this is never tighter overhead than the scheme already shipped.
      //
      // SIX PATTERNS FOR THREE STEPS, AND THE THREE EXTRA ARE NOT DECORATION. A sparse plan writes
      // only what it marks and the space between two layers belongs to nobody, so every row between
      // a step and the ceiling has to be filled or the step hangs below a void. Ring at inset k
      // carries its stair at projection 3-k and needs solid fill at every projection above it:
      // inset 0 needs fill at 1 and 2, inset 1 needs fill at 1, inset 2 needs none because the
      // ceiling plane is already solid. Deleting any fill layer is invisible in the JSON and
      // obvious in game. VaultedHallSchemeTest is the shape of test that catches it.
      //
      // stone_brick_stairs, NOT polished_andesite_stairs, copying vaulted_hall for its reason and
      // not its look: polished andesite ages to andesite and then to GRAVEL, and gravel overhead is
      // falling debris. stone_brick_stairs composes to dirt with no gravel and no air, and the dirt
      // is wanted -- it is the only anchor floor_growth and hanging_growth have.
      //
      // minFloorIndex 1 matches every other stone hall: floor 0 is the mud stratum.
      "name": "deep_vaulted_hall",
      "weight": 10,
      "min_floor_index": 1,
      "min_size": 11,
      "min_height": 8,
      "ceiling": {
        "patterns": [
          {
            "type": "dungeons2:border",
            "projection": 3,
            "config": {
              "block": "minecraft:stone_brick_stairs",
              "inset": 0,
              "orient": "outward",
              "properties": { "half": "top" }
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "dungeons2:border",
            "projection": 2,
            "config": { "block": "minecraft:stone_bricks", "inset": 0 }
          },
